---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="11-debugging"
  lessonSlug="01-spot-the-bug"
  hook="Three broken functions. Each has exactly ONE bug. Find each with the smallest change that fixes it."
>
  <h2>Bug #1 — off-by-one</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def greet_all(names):
    greetings = []
    for i in range(len(names) - 1):           # ← BUG
        greetings.append(f"Hello, {names[i]}!")
    return greetings

print(greet_all(["Ada", "Bob", "Cat"]))     # only 2 greetings — wrong`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Fix">
    <p>
      Drop the <code>- 1</code>, or better: iterate directly with
      <code>for name in names:</code> — more Pythonic.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Bug #2 — function does nothing</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def max_three(a, b, c):
    return c                                  # ← BUG: always returns c

print(max_three(1, 5, 3))   # 3 — should be 5`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Fix">
    <p>
      Add real comparison logic: <code>if a &gt;= b and a &gt;= c: return a</code>
      etc. Or just <code>return max(a, b, c)</code> using the builtin.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Bug #3 — logic inverted</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def count_evens(numbers):
    count = 0
    for n in numbers:
        if n % 2 != 0:                        # ← BUG: counts ODDS
            count += 1
    return count

print(count_evens([1, 3, 5, 7]))   # 4 — should be 0`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Why this bug is sneaky">
    <p>
      For <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]</code> the wrong answer (3 odds) happens
      to equal the right answer (3 evens). The test
      <code>test_count_evens_all_odd</code> with input <code>[1, 3, 5, 7]</code>
      is what exposes it. <strong>Coincidences hide bugs.</strong>
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
